WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:03.900
Longtime fans of "The Witcher" video&nbsp;
games are sure to recognize an early&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:07.800
game character in the third season of&nbsp;
"The Witcher" on Netflix. They'll also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:10.560
notice that things go very differently&nbsp;
for the character this time around.

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:14.880
At the beginning of "The Witcher" Season&nbsp;
3, protagonist Geralt of Rivia encounters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:19.620
a peculiar, refined mercenary who goes by&nbsp;
the moniker Professor. Unfortunately for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:23.400
the bespectacled wannabe assassin, the&nbsp;
meeting is as brief as it is violent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:28.200
as Geralt dispatches the Professor after cutting&nbsp;
through his men with the greatest of ease.

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:31.539
"I importune you. Forgiveness, sir?"

00:00:36.147 --> 00:00:39.540
The Professor's foray into the world&nbsp;
of Netflix's "The Witcher" is so swift&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:43.320
that people who aren't familiar with the&nbsp;
games might wonder why the character is so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:48.360
elaborately designed. The reason is simple: The&nbsp;
antagonist's appearance is a massive Easter egg&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:52.980
for fans who have played the very first "The&nbsp;
Witcher" game from CD Projekt Red in 2007.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:57.120
The Professor makes multiple appearances in&nbsp;
the game, and as players can no doubt attest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:00.600
he's a far bigger challenge for Geralt&nbsp;
than his Netflix version proved to be.

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:04.920
The ease with which Henry Cavill's Geralt&nbsp;
dispatches an enemy who gave his video game&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:10.020
version such trouble is a sign that by the third&nbsp;
season, Netflix's Geralt is the legendary Witcher&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:14.220
at the height of his powers, and could just&nbsp;
be the most powerful Geralt we've seen so far.

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:18.180
That's not to say that the Professor is&nbsp;
anywhere near the strongest antagonist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:21.960
Geralt has to carve his way through in the&nbsp;
games, but the assassin is nevertheless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:26.520
a formidable challenge whom the Witcher&nbsp;
faces more than once. He's a condescending,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:31.620
snide man who isn't afraid to rely on underhanded&nbsp;
tactics like wrist-mounted crossbows and powerful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:36.060
protective spells. The Professor also shows&nbsp;
very little fear while fighting Geralt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:40.020
and isn't shy to lecture the protagonist&nbsp;
about his views on the subject of Witchers.

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:45.960
"You were meant to slay monsters, without making&nbsp;
judgments, philosophizing or nursing doubts.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:47.880
You're ineffective."

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:51.660
Apart from being a brief nod at the people&nbsp;
familiar with the first "Witcher" game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:56.040
the assassin's presence in the Netflix show&nbsp;
is also a fun meta-joke of sorts. Because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.040 --> 00:02:00.360
the Professor is one of the two first major bad&nbsp;
guys Geralt encounters in his very first game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:04.920
with the other being Azar Javed, he can be&nbsp;
viewed as something of a starter villain. Here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:09.180
he's pitted against a considerably stronger&nbsp;
and more experienced Geralt, and the power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:13.320
imbalance between the two is not unlike what&nbsp;
would happen if a video game character returned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:17.820
to the first area of a game after maxing all&nbsp;
their stats. Thanks for dropping by, Professor!

00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:21.900
There's another key reason why the Geralt from&nbsp;
the games may have had a tougher time with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:26.160
Professor, apart from the differences in his sheer&nbsp;
skill and power. In the first "Witcher" game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:30.780
Geralt is dealing with far more than the usual&nbsp;
world-ending threats and man-eating monstrosities;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:34.980
he has also lost his memory. This&nbsp;
Geralt is more or less a blank slate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:38.400
and still a long way away from the&nbsp;
dominant, intimidating fighter he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:41.760
once was or would become again by the time&nbsp;
the third "Witcher" game rolled around.

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:45.360
The live-action Geralt, on the other hand,&nbsp;
might be struggling with his emotions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:50.160
but his memory is fine. So are his fighting&nbsp;
skills, his knowledge of Signs, and all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:54.660
things that make him such a formidable opponent.&nbsp;
This Geralt also meets the Professor in a random,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:58.140
muddy backyard, while the game versions&nbsp;
of the characters contend in situations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.140 --> 00:03:02.630
where the Professor has an upper hand. At one&nbsp;
point, he leads an assault against the Witcher&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.630 --> 00:03:05.880
base Kaer Morhen, and in others,&nbsp;
he's protected by powerful magic.

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:09.900
Still, let's be honest -- even if the&nbsp;
Professor had brought his full bag of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:13.500
tricks in the Netflix show, it's unlikely&nbsp;
that he'd last long against this driven,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:15.840
resourceful, and tougher-than-ever Geralt.

